update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":60,"stem":"These drugs are specified under the UK Drug Driving Regulations 2015, for which police may test a driver suspected of drug-impaired driving:","choices":["Methadone","Fentanyl","Tramadol","Morphine","Oxycodone"],"correct":[true,false,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p>The Drug Driving (Specified Limits) (England and Wales) Regulations 2015 set defined blood concentration limits for a fixed list of drugs, split into two groups: illicit drugs with very low (\"zero tolerance\"-style) limits (e.g. cannabis, cocaine, ketamine, MDMA, LSD, heroin/6-monoacetylmorphine), and a group of commonly prescribed medicines with higher limits reflecting therapeutic use, including methadone, morphine, diazepam, oxazepam, lorazepam, temazepam, flunitrazepam, and clonazepam. Patients prescribed these drugs are not automatically committing an offence provided their driving is not impaired and, if stopped, they can show the medication was prescribed and taken as directed (a statutory \"medical defence\").</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Methadone has a specified legal limit under this legislation, reflecting its common use in opioid substitution therapy.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Fentanyl is not among the drugs with a specified legal limit under the current regulations, despite being a potent opioid with driving-impairment potential — it is not part of the named list.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Tramadol is not included among the drugs with a specified legal limit under this legislation, despite being commonly prescribed and having sedative/psychomotor effects.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Morphine has a specified legal limit, relevant both to patients on prescribed opioid analgesia and to heroin users (heroin is metabolised to morphine, and 6-monoacetylmorphine — a heroin-specific marker — is separately listed with its own very low limit).</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — Oxycodone is not among the drugs with a specified legal limit under this legislation, despite its widespread prescription for pain.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Controlled Drugs Classification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Misuse of Drugs Act 1971: Classes A–C</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Class</th><th>Penalty Tier</th><th>Examples Relevant to Pain Medicine</th><th>Other Notable Examples</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class A</strong></td><td>Highest harm/penalty</td><td>Morphine, diamorphine (heroin), fentanyl, oxycodone, methadone, ketamine (injectable misuse context varies)</td><td>Cocaine, MDMA, LSD, magic mushrooms</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class B</strong></td><td>Intermediate</td><td>Codeine (in unlicensed/high-dose non-medicinal contexts), cannabis, pregabalin, gabapentin</td><td>Amphetamines, barbiturates, synthetic cannabinoids</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class C</strong></td><td>Lowest of the three</td><td>Tramadol, benzodiazepines (e.g. diazepam), zolpidem/zopiclone</td><td>Anabolic steroids, GHB</td></tr>\n        </table><p style=\"margin:10px 0 0;font-size:12.5px;font-style:italic;color:var(--stone)\">Note: Class relates to penalties for unlawful possession/supply, not clinical prescribing rules.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Misuse of Drugs Regulations 2001: Schedules 1–5</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Schedule</th><th>Description</th><th>Prescribing/Storage Requirements</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 1</strong></td><td>No recognised medicinal use</td><td>Possession/supply generally unlawful except under Home Office licence (research only)</td><td>LSD, raw opium (unprocessed), psilocybin</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 2</strong></td><td>Recognised medicinal use, high abuse potential</td><td>Safe custody (locked CD cabinet), CD register entry with running balance, prescription must specify form/strength/dose/total quantity in words and figures</td><td>Morphine, diamorphine, fentanyl, oxycodone, methadone, ketamine, amphetamine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 3</strong></td><td>Recognised medicinal use, lower abuse potential than Sch 2</td><td>CD prescription-writing rules apply (words + figures); most exempt from safe custody (tramadol, midazolam, gabapentin, pregabalin, phenobarbital exempt); no CD register requirement</td><td>Tramadol, midazolam, gabapentin, pregabalin, temazepam, buprenorphine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 4</strong></td><td>Part 1: benzodiazepines; Part 2: anabolic steroids</td><td>No CD prescription-writing requirements; no safe custody; no register</td><td>Diazepam, lorazepam, testosterone</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Schedule 5</strong></td><td>Very low abuse potential preparations</td><td>Minimal restrictions; can be sold OTC in some cases</td><td>Codeine</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_mtf_drug_driving_regs_2015_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":65,"stem":"Ketamine:","choices":["Ketamine is available clinically as a racemic mixture of S and R isomers.","Chronic ketamine use is associated with toxicity to the urinary tract.","The R(-) isomer of ketamine is more potent than the S(+) isomer.","The S(+) isomer is associated with greater psychomimetic effects than the R(-) isomer.","Ketamine has local anaesthetic-like effects via blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels."],"correct":[true,true,false,true,true],"explanation":"<p>Ketamine is a phencyclidine derivative, primarily known for NMDA receptor antagonism producing dissociative anaesthesia and analgesia, though it has several other receptor and channel interactions contributing to its overall clinical effect.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Standard clinical ketamine preparations are a racemic mixture of the S(+) and R(-) enantiomers; a single-isomer S(+)-ketamine (esketamine) preparation is also available separately, roughly twice as potent as the racemate.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Chronic/heavy ketamine use is well recognised to cause urinary tract toxicity, specifically \"ketamine-induced ulcerative cystitis,\" causing severe lower urinary tract symptoms (frequency, urgency, dysuria, haematuria), reduced bladder capacity, and in severe cases hydronephrosis and renal impairment secondary to bladder pathology.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — It is the S(+) isomer, not R(-), that is more potent — the S(+) isomer has higher affinity for the NMDA receptor and is approximately 2–4 times more potent as an analgesic/anaesthetic than the R(-) isomer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — The S(+) isomer has greater psychomimetic activity than the R(-) isomer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. TRUE</strong> — In addition to its principal NMDA receptor antagonism, ketamine also has local anaesthetic-like properties via blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels, contributing to its analgesic effect and providing a pharmacological basis for its occasional use (typically at higher/local concentrations) in modulating neuronal excitability beyond the NMDA pathway alone; ketamine also interacts with opioid receptors, monoaminergic reuptake, and voltage-gated calcium channels, reflecting its broad multimodal pharmacology.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Ketamine Pharmacology</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Classification & Structure</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Phencyclidine (PCP) derivative, arylcyclohexylamine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Stereochemistry</strong></td><td>Chiral molecule with one stereocentre → two enantiomers (S(+) and R(-)), not geometric isomers</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical preparations</strong></td><td>Racemic mixture (50:50 S:R) is standard; single-isomer S(+)-ketamine (esketamine) also available, ~2x more potent than the racemate</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mechanism of Action</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Primary</strong></td><td>Non-competitive (use-dependent, open-channel blocking) antagonist at the NMDA receptor, binding within the channel pore rather than the glutamate binding site</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Muscarinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Antagonist</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Nicotinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Inhibitor</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Opioid receptors</strong></td><td>Weak agonist activity — mu, kappa, sigma sites implicated</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Voltage-gated sodium channels</strong></td><td>Local anaesthetic-like membrane-stabilising effect (NOT potassium channels)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Monoaminergic reuptake</strong></td><td>Inhibition (noradrenaline, serotonin) — central sympathomimetic basis</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Isomer differences</strong></td><td>S(+) has higher NMDA affinity and is more potent than R(-); S(+) is associated with greater psychomimetic/hallucinogenic effects; R(-) is better tolerated psychologically and of research interest (e.g. depression) for separating benefit from dissociative side effects</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Cardiovascular Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Dominant effect</strong></td><td>Central sympathetic stimulation (inhibits catecholamine reuptake) → increased HR, BP, cardiac output</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Masked effect</strong></td><td>Also has a direct myocardial depressant effect, normally masked by sympathetic stimulation; unmasked in catecholamine-depleted states (critical illness, sepsis) → unexpected hypotension/reduced contractility</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>QTc</strong></td><td>No significant QTc prolongation</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Respiratory Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Airway/drive</strong></td><td>Relative preservation of airway reflexes and respiratory drive compared to other anaesthetic agents</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Bronchodilation</strong></td><td>Useful in refractory status asthmaticus (via sympathomimetic + direct bronchial smooth muscle relaxation); no established isomer-specific superiority</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">CNS/Neurological Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Cerebral metabolism</strong></td><td>S(+) ketamine reduces cerebral metabolic rate while preserving cerebrovascular autoregulation and CO2 reactivity (revising older teaching that ketamine universally raises ICP)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Seizures/epilepsy</strong></td><td>Does not reliably trigger clinical seizures; used in refractory status epilepticus; not contraindicated in epilepsy</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Neuroprotection</strong></td><td>Possible neuroprotective/neuroregenerative effects — proposed mechanism is <em>reduction</em> (not increase) of excitotoxic calcium influx/glutamate activity via NMDA blockade; also implicated in synaptogenesis (BDNF/mTOR pathway — relevant to depression research)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical effects</strong></td><td>Causes dissociative anaesthesia, emergence phenomena (hallucinations, vivid dreams), nystagmus</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Renal/Urinary Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Renal failure</strong></td><td>Not contraindicated in chronic renal failure — metabolites are not a major clinical concern (unlike morphine/pethidine)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Chronic/heavy use</strong></td><td>Causes ketamine-induced ulcerative cystitis — urinary frequency, urgency, dysuria, haematuria, reduced bladder capacity, and in severe cases hydronephrosis/renal impairment</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Immune Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Immunomodulation</strong></td><td>Anti-inflammatory properties — attenuates pro-inflammatory cytokine release (TNF-alpha, IL-6); relevance to sepsis and perioperative inflammatory response is an active research area</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Metabolism</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Pathway</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily via CYP3A4 (and CYP2B6) → norketamine (active metabolite, ~1/3–1/5 potency of parent)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal excretion of metabolites</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_mtf_ketamine_general_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":66,"stem":"Regarding ketamine, are the following statements true or false?","choices":["It acts on the central nervous system.","It has local anaesthetic properties.","S(+) ketamine is four times as potent as the racemate.","S(+) ketamine and R(-) ketamine have the same affinity for NMDA receptors.","Ketamine has two geometric isomers."],"correct":[true,true,false,false,false],"explanation":"<p>Ketamine is a phencyclidine derivative acting primarily as a non-competitive NMDA receptor antagonist, producing dissociative anaesthesia, analgesia, and a range of other CNS and peripheral effects, alongside a distinct pharmacokinetic and isomer-specific profile.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine acts centrally, principally via NMDA receptor antagonism in the CNS, producing its characteristic dissociative anaesthetic, analgesic, amnesic, and psychomimetic effects; it also has effects at opioid, monoaminergic, and muscarinic receptors within the CNS.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine has local anaesthetic-like properties via blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels, contributing an additional analgesic mechanism beyond its central NMDA antagonism.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — S(+) ketamine is approximately twice as potent as the racemate (not four times), reflecting that the racemate is a 50:50 mix of the more potent S(+) and less potent R(-) isomers — S(+) alone therefore has roughly double the potency of the mixed preparation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. FALSE</strong> — S(+) and R(-) ketamine do not have the same NMDA receptor affinity — S(+) ketamine has a higher affinity for the NMDA receptor (and is more potent) than R(-) ketamine, which underlies the difference in their clinical potency and side-effect profiles.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — Ketamine''s S(+) and R(-) forms are enantiomers (optical/stereoisomers), arising from a single chiral centre, not geometric (cis-trans) isomers — geometric isomerism requires restricted rotation (e.g. around a double bond or ring), which is not the basis of ketamine''s isomerism.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Ketamine Pharmacology</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Classification & Structure</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Phencyclidine (PCP) derivative, arylcyclohexylamine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Stereochemistry</strong></td><td>Chiral molecule with one stereocentre → two enantiomers (S(+) and R(-)), not geometric isomers</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical preparations</strong></td><td>Racemic mixture (50:50 S:R) is standard; single-isomer S(+)-ketamine (esketamine) also available, ~2x more potent than the racemate</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mechanism of Action</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Primary</strong></td><td>Non-competitive (use-dependent, open-channel blocking) antagonist at the NMDA receptor, binding within the channel pore rather than the glutamate binding site</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Muscarinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Antagonist</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Nicotinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Inhibitor</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Opioid receptors</strong></td><td>Weak agonist activity — mu, kappa, sigma sites implicated</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Voltage-gated sodium channels</strong></td><td>Local anaesthetic-like membrane-stabilising effect (NOT potassium channels)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Monoaminergic reuptake</strong></td><td>Inhibition (noradrenaline, serotonin) — central sympathomimetic basis</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Isomer differences</strong></td><td>S(+) has higher NMDA affinity and is more potent than R(-); S(+) is associated with greater psychomimetic/hallucinogenic effects; R(-) is better tolerated psychologically and of research interest (e.g. depression) for separating benefit from dissociative side effects</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Cardiovascular Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Dominant effect</strong></td><td>Central sympathetic stimulation (inhibits catecholamine reuptake) → increased HR, BP, cardiac output</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Masked effect</strong></td><td>Also has a direct myocardial depressant effect, normally masked by sympathetic stimulation; unmasked in catecholamine-depleted states (critical illness, sepsis) → unexpected hypotension/reduced contractility</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>QTc</strong></td><td>No significant QTc prolongation</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Respiratory Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Airway/drive</strong></td><td>Relative preservation of airway reflexes and respiratory drive compared to other anaesthetic agents</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Bronchodilation</strong></td><td>Useful in refractory status asthmaticus (via sympathomimetic + direct bronchial smooth muscle relaxation); no established isomer-specific superiority</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">CNS/Neurological Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Cerebral metabolism</strong></td><td>S(+) ketamine reduces cerebral metabolic rate while preserving cerebrovascular autoregulation and CO2 reactivity (revising older teaching that ketamine universally raises ICP)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Seizures/epilepsy</strong></td><td>Does not reliably trigger clinical seizures; used in refractory status epilepticus; not contraindicated in epilepsy</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Neuroprotection</strong></td><td>Possible neuroprotective/neuroregenerative effects — proposed mechanism is <em>reduction</em> (not increase) of excitotoxic calcium influx/glutamate activity via NMDA blockade; also implicated in synaptogenesis (BDNF/mTOR pathway — relevant to depression research)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical effects</strong></td><td>Causes dissociative anaesthesia, emergence phenomena (hallucinations, vivid dreams), nystagmus</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Renal/Urinary Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Renal failure</strong></td><td>Not contraindicated in chronic renal failure — metabolites are not a major clinical concern (unlike morphine/pethidine)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Chronic/heavy use</strong></td><td>Causes ketamine-induced ulcerative cystitis — urinary frequency, urgency, dysuria, haematuria, reduced bladder capacity, and in severe cases hydronephrosis/renal impairment</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Immune Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Immunomodulation</strong></td><td>Anti-inflammatory properties — attenuates pro-inflammatory cytokine release (TNF-alpha, IL-6); relevance to sepsis and perioperative inflammatory response is an active research area</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Metabolism</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Pathway</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily via CYP3A4 (and CYP2B6) → norketamine (active metabolite, ~1/3–1/5 potency of parent)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal excretion of metabolites</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_mtf_ketamine_isomer_potency_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":67,"stem":"Ketamine binds to:","choices":["NMDA receptors.","Muscarinic receptors.","Nicotinic receptors.","Opioid receptors.","Neuronal potassium channels, to exhibit its local anaesthetic properties."],"correct":[true,true,true,true,false],"explanation":"<p>Ketamine has a broad receptor and channel interaction profile beyond its principal NMDA antagonism, which underlies much of its diverse clinical effect and side-effect spectrum.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine''s principal mechanism is non-competitive antagonism at the NMDA receptor, binding within the open ion channel pore, underlying its dissociative anaesthetic and analgesic effects.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine has affinity for muscarinic acetylcholine receptors (antagonist activity), contributing to some of its anticholinergic-type effects and possibly its emergence phenomena/delirium profile.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine also inhibits nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, adding to its broad receptor interaction profile beyond the NMDA system.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine has weak affinity at opioid receptors (mu, kappa, and sigma sites have all been implicated), contributing modestly to its overall analgesic effect alongside NMDA antagonism.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — Ketamine''s local anaesthetic-like properties arise from blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels, not potassium channels — this sodium channel effect underlies its use-dependent membrane-stabilising action, contributing an additional analgesic mechanism distinct from NMDA antagonism.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Ketamine Pharmacology</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Classification & Structure</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Phencyclidine (PCP) derivative, arylcyclohexylamine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Stereochemistry</strong></td><td>Chiral molecule with one stereocentre → two enantiomers (S(+) and R(-)), not geometric isomers</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical preparations</strong></td><td>Racemic mixture (50:50 S:R) is standard; single-isomer S(+)-ketamine (esketamine) also available, ~2x more potent than the racemate</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mechanism of Action</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Primary</strong></td><td>Non-competitive (use-dependent, open-channel blocking) antagonist at the NMDA receptor, binding within the channel pore rather than the glutamate binding site</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Muscarinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Antagonist</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Nicotinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Inhibitor</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Opioid receptors</strong></td><td>Weak agonist activity — mu, kappa, sigma sites implicated</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Voltage-gated sodium channels</strong></td><td>Local anaesthetic-like membrane-stabilising effect (NOT potassium channels)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Monoaminergic reuptake</strong></td><td>Inhibition (noradrenaline, serotonin) — central sympathomimetic basis</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Isomer differences</strong></td><td>S(+) has higher NMDA affinity and is more potent than R(-); S(+) is associated with greater psychomimetic/hallucinogenic effects; R(-) is better tolerated psychologically and of research interest (e.g. depression) for separating benefit from dissociative side effects</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Cardiovascular Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Dominant effect</strong></td><td>Central sympathetic stimulation (inhibits catecholamine reuptake) → increased HR, BP, cardiac output</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Masked effect</strong></td><td>Also has a direct myocardial depressant effect, normally masked by sympathetic stimulation; unmasked in catecholamine-depleted states (critical illness, sepsis) → unexpected hypotension/reduced contractility</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>QTc</strong></td><td>No significant QTc prolongation</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Respiratory Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Airway/drive</strong></td><td>Relative preservation of airway reflexes and respiratory drive compared to other anaesthetic agents</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Bronchodilation</strong></td><td>Useful in refractory status asthmaticus (via sympathomimetic + direct bronchial smooth muscle relaxation); no established isomer-specific superiority</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">CNS/Neurological Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Cerebral metabolism</strong></td><td>S(+) ketamine reduces cerebral metabolic rate while preserving cerebrovascular autoregulation and CO2 reactivity (revising older teaching that ketamine universally raises ICP)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Seizures/epilepsy</strong></td><td>Does not reliably trigger clinical seizures; used in refractory status epilepticus; not contraindicated in epilepsy</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Neuroprotection</strong></td><td>Possible neuroprotective/neuroregenerative effects — proposed mechanism is <em>reduction</em> (not increase) of excitotoxic calcium influx/glutamate activity via NMDA blockade; also implicated in synaptogenesis (BDNF/mTOR pathway — relevant to depression research)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical effects</strong></td><td>Causes dissociative anaesthesia, emergence phenomena (hallucinations, vivid dreams), nystagmus</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Renal/Urinary Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Renal failure</strong></td><td>Not contraindicated in chronic renal failure — metabolites are not a major clinical concern (unlike morphine/pethidine)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Chronic/heavy use</strong></td><td>Causes ketamine-induced ulcerative cystitis — urinary frequency, urgency, dysuria, haematuria, reduced bladder capacity, and in severe cases hydronephrosis/renal impairment</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Immune Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Immunomodulation</strong></td><td>Anti-inflammatory properties — attenuates pro-inflammatory cytokine release (TNF-alpha, IL-6); relevance to sepsis and perioperative inflammatory response is an active research area</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Metabolism</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Pathway</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily via CYP3A4 (and CYP2B6) → norketamine (active metabolite, ~1/3–1/5 potency of parent)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal excretion of metabolites</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_mtf_ketamine_receptor_binding_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-04","rotationIndex":68,"stem":"Ketamine:","choices":["S(+) ketamine reduces cerebral metabolism and maintains cerebrovascular autoregulation.","Ketamine triggers seizure activity.","Ketamine is contraindicated in patients with epilepsy.","Ketamine may have neuroprotective and neuroregenerative effects.","Neuroprotection appears to be due to increased calcium fluxes and glutamate accumulation at the NMDA receptor."],"correct":[true,false,false,true,false],"explanation":"<p>Ketamine''s neurological effects have been historically over-simplified and misunderstood — older teaching emphasised raised intracranial pressure and epileptogenic risk, but more recent evidence has substantially revised this view, particularly for the S(+) isomer.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. TRUE</strong> — S(+) ketamine, unlike the racemate at higher doses, has been shown to reduce cerebral metabolic rate while preserving cerebrovascular autoregulation and CO2 reactivity, challenging the older dogma that all ketamine formulations uniformly raise cerebral metabolism and intracranial pressure.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. FALSE</strong> — Ketamine does not reliably trigger clinical seizure activity. While it can produce excitatory EEG changes (particularly in limbic structures) at a neurophysiological level, this does not translate into a clinically significant proconvulsant effect, and ketamine is in fact used as a treatment option in refractory status epilepticus.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. FALSE</strong> — Ketamine is not contraindicated in patients with epilepsy. Given its use in treating refractory status epilepticus and lack of reliable proconvulsant clinical effect, it can be used cautiously in this population, in contrast to older teaching that avoided ketamine in epilepsy.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. TRUE</strong> — Ketamine may have neuroprotective and neuroregenerative effects, an area of active research interest — proposed mechanisms include reduction of excitotoxic injury via NMDA antagonism, anti-inflammatory effects, and (in psychiatric/depression research) promotion of synaptogenesis via downstream BDNF/mTOR pathway activation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. FALSE</strong> — The proposed mechanism of neuroprotection is the opposite of what''s stated — ketamine''s NMDA receptor antagonism reduces excessive calcium influx and glutamate-mediated excitotoxicity (the process that causes neuronal injury in ischaemia/trauma), rather than increasing it; this reduction in excitotoxic calcium/glutamate activity is the proposed protective mechanism.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Ketamine Pharmacology</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Classification & Structure</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Class</strong></td><td>Phencyclidine (PCP) derivative, arylcyclohexylamine</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Stereochemistry</strong></td><td>Chiral molecule with one stereocentre → two enantiomers (S(+) and R(-)), not geometric isomers</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical preparations</strong></td><td>Racemic mixture (50:50 S:R) is standard; single-isomer S(+)-ketamine (esketamine) also available, ~2x more potent than the racemate</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Mechanism of Action</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Target</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Primary</strong></td><td>Non-competitive (use-dependent, open-channel blocking) antagonist at the NMDA receptor, binding within the channel pore rather than the glutamate binding site</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Muscarinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Antagonist</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Nicotinic ACh receptors</strong></td><td>Inhibitor</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Opioid receptors</strong></td><td>Weak agonist activity — mu, kappa, sigma sites implicated</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Voltage-gated sodium channels</strong></td><td>Local anaesthetic-like membrane-stabilising effect (NOT potassium channels)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Monoaminergic reuptake</strong></td><td>Inhibition (noradrenaline, serotonin) — central sympathomimetic basis</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Isomer differences</strong></td><td>S(+) has higher NMDA affinity and is more potent than R(-); S(+) is associated with greater psychomimetic/hallucinogenic effects; R(-) is better tolerated psychologically and of research interest (e.g. depression) for separating benefit from dissociative side effects</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Cardiovascular Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Dominant effect</strong></td><td>Central sympathetic stimulation (inhibits catecholamine reuptake) → increased HR, BP, cardiac output</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Masked effect</strong></td><td>Also has a direct myocardial depressant effect, normally masked by sympathetic stimulation; unmasked in catecholamine-depleted states (critical illness, sepsis) → unexpected hypotension/reduced contractility</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>QTc</strong></td><td>No significant QTc prolongation</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Respiratory Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Airway/drive</strong></td><td>Relative preservation of airway reflexes and respiratory drive compared to other anaesthetic agents</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Bronchodilation</strong></td><td>Useful in refractory status asthmaticus (via sympathomimetic + direct bronchial smooth muscle relaxation); no established isomer-specific superiority</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">CNS/Neurological Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Cerebral metabolism</strong></td><td>S(+) ketamine reduces cerebral metabolic rate while preserving cerebrovascular autoregulation and CO2 reactivity (revising older teaching that ketamine universally raises ICP)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Seizures/epilepsy</strong></td><td>Does not reliably trigger clinical seizures; used in refractory status epilepticus; not contraindicated in epilepsy</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Neuroprotection</strong></td><td>Possible neuroprotective/neuroregenerative effects — proposed mechanism is <em>reduction</em> (not increase) of excitotoxic calcium influx/glutamate activity via NMDA blockade; also implicated in synaptogenesis (BDNF/mTOR pathway — relevant to depression research)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Clinical effects</strong></td><td>Causes dissociative anaesthesia, emergence phenomena (hallucinations, vivid dreams), nystagmus</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Renal/Urinary Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Renal failure</strong></td><td>Not contraindicated in chronic renal failure — metabolites are not a major clinical concern (unlike morphine/pethidine)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Chronic/heavy use</strong></td><td>Causes ketamine-induced ulcerative cystitis — urinary frequency, urgency, dysuria, haematuria, reduced bladder capacity, and in severe cases hydronephrosis/renal impairment</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Immune Effects</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Immunomodulation</strong></td><td>Anti-inflammatory properties — attenuates pro-inflammatory cytokine release (TNF-alpha, IL-6); relevance to sepsis and perioperative inflammatory response is an active research area</td></tr>\n        </table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong);\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Metabolism</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Pathway</strong></td><td>Hepatic, primarily via CYP3A4 (and CYP2B6) → norketamine (active metabolite, ~1/3–1/5 potency of parent)</td></tr>\n        <tr><td><strong>Excretion</strong></td><td>Renal excretion of metabolites</td></tr>\n        </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_mtf_ketamine_neurological_effects_001';
